            "lens": "review-risk",
            "findings": [
              {
                "id": "R1-lock-nb-poll",
                "lens": "risk",
                "location": "engine/review/fsstore/lock_unix.go:44-60",
                "severity": "SUGGESTION",
                "claim": "The lock polls LOCK_NB every 50ms instead of blocking, so waiters are not served in FIFO order and a continuously contending caller could in theory starve. The 30s deadline bounds this and fails closed with ErrLockHeld (nothing stored), so no exploitable round-bypass results.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/review/fsstore/lock_unix.go:44-60"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed all 18 changed paths in the patch.",
              "The lock falls back safely: on flock errors other than EWOULDBLOCK (ENOLCK, EOPNOTSUPP) it returns an error, so a blocking verdict is not stored without a lock; timeout also fails closed.",
              "lockByPath opens with O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY and verifies os.SameFile against the walked directory, so a symlink swap cannot redirect the lock; the linux Lock path keeps the held-descriptor walk.",
              "On Windows no lock is held; this is documented, and the baseline had the same behavior for non-linux, so it is not a regression.",
              "No secrets, dependency changes, or unsafe input handling were introduced; the remaining changes are tests, docs and a defensive require() check."
            ]
          }
        }
